arXiv:2602.21039v1 [stat.ML] 24 Feb 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: 
<br class="ltx_break">Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Rafael Hanashiro
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>MIT
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rafah@mit.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Abhishek Shetty
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>MIT
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">shetty@mit.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Patrick Jaillet
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>MIT
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">jaillet@mit.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Towards understanding the statistical complexity of learning from heterogeneous sources, we study the problem of multi-distribution learning. Given <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> data sources, the goal is to output a classifier for each source by exploiting shared structure to reduce sample complexity. We focus on the bounded label noise setting to determine whether the fast <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> rates achievable in single-task learning extend to this regime with minimal dependence on <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Surprisingly, we show that this is not the case. We demonstrate that learning across <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> distributions inherently incurs slow rates scaling with <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k/\epsilon^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k/\epsilon^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, even under constant noise levels, unless each distribution is learned separately. A key technical contribution is a structured hypothesis-testing framework that captures the statistical cost of certifying near-optimality under bounded noise—a cost we show is unavoidable in the multi-distribution setting.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">Finally, we prove that when competing with the stronger benchmark of each distribution’s optimal Bayes error, the sample complexity incurs a <span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multiplicative</span> penalty in <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. This establishes a <span id="abstract1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">statistical</span> separation between random classification noise and Massart noise, highlighting a fundamental barrier unique to learning from multiple sources.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Data heterogeneity inherent in learning from multiple sources is a fundamental challenge in modern machine learning. In federated learning, data resides across numerous devices or institutions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>; in algorithmic fairness, models must perform well across diverse demographic groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>; in domain adaptation, one must reason about distinct but related distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>; and in language modeling, the composition of pretraining data sources is critical. These applications have sparked significant interest in understanding how shared structure can be leveraged to minimize data requirements, with the ideal goal being that learning across heterogeneous sources incurs only a mild statistical overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This motivates the study of <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multi-Distribution Learning (MDL)</span>, where a learner must perform well across <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> distributions simultaneously. Remarkably, in both the realizable and agnostic settings, MDL admits sample complexities of <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, for classes of VC dimension <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. This reflects only an <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">additive</span> overhead in <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> relative to single-distribution learning, suggesting that in these classical settings, learning from heterogeneous sources is essentially “as easy as PAC learning.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, the realizable and agnostic settings represent extremes of label quality. A more realistic scenario involves labels that are corrupted, but not entirely adversarially. This is captured by the <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bounded noise</span> model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, which assumes there exists a function <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> such that<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          We will often write <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\mathinner{\left(\mathinner{\left(X,Y\right)}\in A\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\mathinner{\left(\mathinner{\left(X,Y\right)}\in A\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> as shorthand for <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{\mathinner{\left(X,Y\right)}\sim P}\mathinner{\left(\mathinner{\left(X,Y\right)}\in A\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{\mathinner{\left(X,Y\right)}\sim P}\mathinner{\left(\mathinner{\left(X,Y\right)}\in A\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y|X=x\right)}\leq\eta&lt;\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y|X=x\right)}\leq\eta&lt;\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bayes classifier</span> and attains the minimum possible error. In the standard single-distribution PAC setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, empirical risk minimization (ERM) achieves a sample complexity of <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, if we treat <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> as constant, this recovers the fast <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> realizable rate. Given the precedent that MDL incurs only a small overhead in <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, one might hope that these fast rates persist under label noise as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we answer this question in the negative: we show that under bounded label noise, the cost of handling multiple distributions can be significantly higher than in the single-distribution setting.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notation.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left[n\right]}\coloneqq\mathinner{\left\{1,2,\dots,n\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left[n\right]}\coloneqq\mathinner{\left\{1,2,\dots,n\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\llbracket n\rrbracket\coloneqq\mathinner{\left\{0,1,\dots,n\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\llbracket n\rrbracket\coloneqq\mathinner{\left\{0,1,\dots,n\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lesssim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≲</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lesssim</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gtrsim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gtrsim</annotation></semantics></math> to denote inequalities up to universal constants, and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O},\tilde{\Omega},\tilde{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O},\tilde{\Omega},\tilde{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to hide logarithmic factors. For a distribution <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a function <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the classification error <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\coloneqq P\mathinner{\left(f\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\coloneqq P\mathinner{\left(f\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Problem Setup</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assume sample access to <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> distributions <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\dots,P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\dots,P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and assume there exists a <span id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">shared</span> Bayes classifier <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> such that, for each <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{i}\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\middle|X=x\right)}\leq\eta_{i}&lt;\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{i}\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\middle|X=x\right)}\leq\eta_{i}&lt;\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We assume that the noise upper bounds <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{1},\dots,\eta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{1},\dots,\eta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are known, but the optimal errors <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}^{*}\coloneqq\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P_{i}\right)}\leq\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}^{*}\coloneqq\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P_{i}\right)}\leq\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are unknown. Note that setting all <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> recovers the standard realizable setting, whereas removing the existence assumption on <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> yields the agnostic regime.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to adaptively sample from the <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and output decisions <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{1},\dots,\hat{f}_{k}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{1},\dots,\hat{f}_{k}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> that compete with prescribed benchmarks <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{OPT}_{1},\dots,\mathsf{OPT}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖮𝖯𝖳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝖮𝖯𝖳</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{OPT}_{1},\dots,\mathsf{OPT}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, in the sense that</p>
<table id="S1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\mathinner{\left(\max_{i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}}\mathinner{\left\{\err\mathinner{\left(\hat{f}_{i};P_{i}\right)}-\mathsf{OPT}_{i}\right\}}\leq\epsilon\right)}\geq 1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝖮𝖯𝖳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\mathinner{\left(\max_{i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}}\mathinner{\left\{\err\mathinner{\left(\hat{f}_{i};P_{i}\right)}-\mathsf{OPT}_{i}\right\}}\leq\epsilon\right)}\geq 1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This is known as the <span id="S1.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">personalized</span> setting, as opposed to <span id="S1.SS1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">centralized</span>, since we are allowed to output a different decision per distribution. We focus on three separate regimes.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RCN.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that under RCN, the pointwise error is constant, <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\middle|X=x\right)}=\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\middle|X=x\right)}=\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, implying the optimal error is exactly <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Motivated by this, we introduce the benchmark:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{OPT}_{i}=\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖮𝖯𝖳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{OPT}_{i}=\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(MDL-RCN)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, the learner aims to compete with the known upper bounds <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, even though the true optimal errors <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> may be strictly smaller. We retain distinct indices <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for completeness, but note that their heterogeneity does not fundamentally alter the hardness of the problem; one may effectively treat them as equal.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Minimax.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The standard benchmark in MDL is the minimax risk <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}\coloneqq\max_{i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}}\eta_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}\coloneqq\max_{i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}}\eta_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to the best worst-case error a single hypothesis can achieve across all distributions. We adopt this as our second benchmark:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{OPT}_{i}=\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖮𝖯𝖳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{OPT}_{i}=\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(MDL-MM)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Massart.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we consider the most fine-grained objective, where the learner must compete with the true optimal error of each distribution individually:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{OPT}_{i}=\eta_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖮𝖯𝖳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{OPT}_{i}=\eta_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(MDL-Mass)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Unlike the minimax setting, this requires the learner to adapt to the specific noise level of every distribution. Evidently, this variant is at least as hard as the other two, as the learner targets the strictest possible benchmark for each instance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For any regime, we say that an algorithm <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> has sample complexity <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}^{2k+2}\to\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}^{2k+2}\to\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> if, given any instance <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(\mathcal{F},P_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\eta^{*}_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\eta_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\epsilon,\delta\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(\mathcal{F},P_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\eta^{*}_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\eta_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\epsilon,\delta\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, it satisfies (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) using at most <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\eta_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k}^{*},\eta_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\epsilon,\delta\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\eta_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k}^{*},\eta_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\epsilon,\delta\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> samples in total across all distributions. We omit parameters from the input when there is no dependence on them. For convenience, we additionally define <math id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\coloneqq\max_{i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}}\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\coloneqq\max_{i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}}\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Overview and Contributions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a near-complete characterization of the sample complexity for MDL under bounded label noise. Along the way, we introduce a structured hypothesis testing problem that may be of independent interest.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Upper Bounds (<a href="#S3" title="3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a meta-algorithm (<a href="#alg1" title="In 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) for the first two variants, following the iterative approach of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. In each round, the algorithm learns at least half of the active distributions, subsequently identifying them via a testing subroutine. Under (<a href="#S1.Ex3" title="Equation MDL-RCN ‣ RCN. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-RCN</span></a>), the target <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are known, allowing for direct certification of optimality. This yields an upper bound</p>
<table id="S1.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{\epsilon+\eta_{i}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{\epsilon+\eta_{i}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 (() upper bound). ‣ 3.3 () Upper Bound ‣ 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The minimax variant is more subtle, as the benchmark <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown. To address this, we devise a procedure that estimates <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in increments and incurs only a logarithmic overhead. This results in an (<a href="#S1.Ex4" title="Equation MDL-MM ‣ Minimax. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-MM</span></a>) upper bound</p>
<table id="S1.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\epsilon+\eta^{*}\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\epsilon+\eta^{*}\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (() upper bound). ‣ 3.4 () Upper Bound ‣ 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In both settings, the first term reflects the statistical cost of learning, while the second captures the testing complexity. Since the latter scales quadratically with <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, it can be preferable to learn each distribution independently, which would require <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta_{i}\right)}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta_{i}\right)}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> samples. <a href="#alg1" title="In 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> explicitly compares these regimes (via an initial check) and switches to per-distribution learning whenever it is more sample-efficient. Note that in the realizable case (<math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>), we recover the rate <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The testing component arises naturally from the learning objective, as the ability to learn an optimal classifier intuitively implies the ability to verify its performance. However, this certification incurs a statistical cost of <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{\epsilon+\eta}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{\epsilon+\eta}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> to reliably distinguish between noise rates <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Establishing the necessity of this cost constitutes the more challenging direction. Our lower bounds confirm that it is not an artifact of analysis, but a fundamental barrier in learning under bounded noise.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Structured Hypothesis Testing (<a href="#S4" title="4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed, the ability to test whether a classifier is optimal is intrinsic to the learning problem. To formalize this task, we introduce <span id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Structured Hypothesis Testing</span> (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>): given a fixed function, decide whether it is <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal under RCN with known noise <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1/4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This problem admits two natural strategies: (i) directly thresholding the empirical errors, or (ii) first learning <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to compare against the candidate. The former succeeds with <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon^{2}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon^{2}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> samples, while the latter requires <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. We demonstrate that this trade-off is fundamental by establishing a matching lower bound, yielding the optimal sample complexity:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (() upper bound). ‣ Learning-Augmented Testing. ‣ 4.1 () Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (() lower bound). ‣ 4.2 () Lower Bound ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We then extend this paradigm to <span id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multi-Distribution Structured Hypothesis Testing</span> (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>), where the goal is to solve <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneous instances of (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) across distributions <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\dots,P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\dots,P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> that share a Bayes classifier with fixed noise rate <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1/4</annotation></semantics></math>. We prove that the naive strategy of testing each distribution separately is, in fact, optimal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To establish the lower bound, we reduce (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) to (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) by embedding a hard (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) instance into a specific <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, while setting the remaining distributions to the null hypothesis. Since the index <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown, the (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) algorithm is forced to sample sufficiently from all distributions to locate and solve the embedded instance. Crucially, we construct the (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) hard instance such that extending it to multiple distributions preserves the VC dimension of the underlying hypothesis class. This yields the final sample complexity:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5 (() upper bound). ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (() lower bound). ‣ 4.2 () Lower Bound ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MDL Lower Bounds (<a href="#S5" title="5 MDL Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed, testing optimality is a necessary condition for learning. In <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 5.1 (MDL to MSHT upper bound). ‣ 5 MDL Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, we formalize this by showing that any MDL algorithm can be used to solve (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) with an additional cost of only <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(k/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(k/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> samples. Consequently, in the fixed-noise regime where <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}_{i}=\eta_{i}=1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}_{i}=\eta_{i}=1/4</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>, every MDL variant requires</p>
<table id="S1.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Omega\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon}+k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Omega\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon}+k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(<a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (MDL lower bound). ‣ 5 MDL Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">samples. When <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\lesssim 1/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\lesssim 1/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, this becomes <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\mathinner{\left(dk/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\mathinner{\left(dk/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>—no better than learning each distribution separately. This establishes optimality of our algorithms for (<a href="#S1.Ex3" title="Equation MDL-RCN ‣ RCN. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-RCN</span></a>) and (<a href="#S1.Ex4" title="Equation MDL-MM ‣ Minimax. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-MM</span></a>), and answers our motivating question in the negative.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Separation Between RCN and Massart (<a href="#S6" title="6 () Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the benchmark in (<a href="#S1.Ex5" title="Equation MDL-Mass ‣ Massart. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-Mass</span></a>), where each output <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> must compete with the <span id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unknown</span> Bayes error <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we prove the stronger lower bound</p>
<table id="S1.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Omega\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon}+k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}+\frac{k\sqrt{d}}{\epsilon}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Omega\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon}+k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}+\frac{k\sqrt{d}}{\epsilon}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(<a href="#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 6.2 (() lower bound). ‣ 6.2 Lower Bound ‣ 6 () Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">under the assumption that <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}\leq 0.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}\leq 0.49</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>. Beyond the learning term <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and the intrinsic testing cost <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{1/\epsilon^{2},\,d/\epsilon\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{1/\epsilon^{2},\,d/\epsilon\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> already necessary in the fixed-noise regime, we show an additional penalty of <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\sqrt{d}/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\sqrt{d}/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> that is unique to this Massart objective. Informally, when the target <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is not given, an MDL learner must certify near-optimality relative to an <span id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unknown</span> baseline on each distribution, and this extra uncertainty forces a <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-scaled sample overhead for each of the <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> tasks. Consequently, while RCN and Massart noise are statistically comparable in the single-distribution setting, they become separable in MDL: competing with <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly harder and necessarily amplifies the dependence on <math id="S1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. This separation has long been known computationally and is closely tied to distribution shift <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. We interpret the present statistical separation in MDL as shedding new light on this phenomenon.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-Distribution Learning.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-distribution learning was introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> under the name <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">collaborative PAC learning</span>. In the realizable case, they proved a tight sample complexity of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> under both personalized and centralized settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the centralized realizable setting, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> improved the logarithmic dependence, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> also gave strategies that compete with a constant multiple of the minimax error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For centralized agnostic MDL, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> proposed a general algorithmic framework based on playing online learning strategies against each other to reach an approximate Nash equilibrium. They obtained a tight rate of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(\frac{\log\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{F}\right\rvert}+k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(\frac{\log\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{F}\right\rvert}+k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> for finite classes and, for hypothesis classes of VC dimension <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, an upper bound of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{dk}{\epsilon}+\frac{d+k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{dk}{\epsilon}+\frac{d+k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>; for the VC case they also proved a lower bound of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Omega}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Omega}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> asked whether this VC lower bound is tight for centralized agnostic MDL. They also provided an algorithm for personalized agnostic MDL with complexity <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and an algorithm for centralized agnostic MDL with complexity <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon^{4}}+\frac{k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon^{4}}+\frac{k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. This question was later answered affirmatively by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, who gave algorithms for centralized agnostic MDL with sample complexity <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, matching the lower bound up to logarithmic factors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Closest to our setting, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> studied a weaker form of realizability where a small subset of classifiers achieves small error: they assume there exist <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}^{*},\dots,f_{m}^{*}\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}^{*},\dots,f_{m}^{*}\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}}\min_{j\in\mathinner{\left[m\right]}}\err\mathinner{\left(f_{j}^{*};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}}\min_{j\in\mathinner{\left[m\right]}}\err\mathinner{\left(f_{j}^{*};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. They gave an algorithm with sample complexity <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{md}{\epsilon}+\frac{k}{\epsilon}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{md}{\epsilon}+\frac{k}{\epsilon}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and showed it is tight up to log factors. In particular, when different distributions are realized by different classifiers, a learner must in the worst case learn each distribution separately; in contrast, we assume a <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">shared</span> Bayes classifier, a structural distinction that enables information sharing not possible in the setting of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Related Learning Settings.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MDL framework has also been studied under a variety of structural assumptions—including convexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, sparsity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, and bounded suboptimality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>—as well as through alternative lenses such as label complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> and the role of adaptivity in sample complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Beyond these theoretical directions, distributionally robust objectives have also been adopted in language models to improve performance across diverse data sources <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On the computational side, a complementary line of work studies the cost of making statistically optimal procedures efficient: since the optimal strategies are inherently randomized, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> showed that derandomizing them is computationally hard. Related notions of learning across heterogeneous groups have also been investigated in other learning frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">PAC Learning.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Statistical learning theory has long been developed through the lens of PAC learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Classical formulations focus either on the realizable case (zero noise) or on the fully agnostic case (no assumptions on the data-generating process). To model intermediate regimes, subsequent works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> introduced explicit assumptions on the label noise, which can often lead to faster learning rates; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> for background.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we focus on the RCN and Massart noise models. From a statistical perspective, the distinction between the two is minor; both admit fast rates. Computationally, however, the Massart generalization proves substantially more challenging. A polynomial-time algorithm for RCN was given by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, whereas a distribution-independent equivalent for Massart noise remained elusive until much later <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, current polynomial-time approaches generally compete only with the noise upper bound, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> established a super-polynomial statistical query SQ lower bound for competing with the Bayes error.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>MDL Upper Bounds</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we develop strategies for (<a href="#S1.Ex3" title="Equation MDL-RCN ‣ RCN. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-RCN</span></a>) and (<a href="#S1.Ex4" title="Equation MDL-MM ‣ Minimax. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-MM</span></a>). Let <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> denote the VC dimension of the hypothesis class <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. <a href="#alg1" title="In 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> outlines the general template for our approach. At a high level, the learner proceeds in round <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Active Set Maintenance:</span> The learner maintains a set of distributions <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> that still need to be learned, initialized with the full set <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(0)}\coloneqq\mathinner{\left\{P_{1},\dots,P_{k}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(0)}\coloneqq\mathinner{\left\{P_{1},\dots,P_{k}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Statistical Learning:</span> It learns a hypothesis <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the uniform mixture over the current active set, denoted by <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}</annotation></semantics></math>, using an ERM oracle (Lines <a href="#alg1.l9" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>–<a href="#alg1.l10" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ERM_{\mathcal{F}}\mathinner{\left(S\right)}\in\argmin_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\mathinner{\left\{\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}\coloneqq\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}}\sum_{\mathinner{\left(x,y\right)}\in S}\mathbb{I}\mathinner{\left\{f\mathinner{\left(x\right)}\neq y\right\}}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ERM</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ERM_{\mathcal{F}}\mathinner{\left(S\right)}\in\argmin_{f\in\mathcal{F}}\mathinner{\left\{\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}\coloneqq\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}}\sum_{\mathinner{\left(x,y\right)}\in S}\mathbb{I}\mathinner{\left\{f\mathinner{\left(x\right)}\neq y\right\}}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Testing:</span> It invokes a <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Test" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Test</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Test</annotation></semantics></math> subroutine (specific to each task) to identify the distributions on which <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> performs well. These distributions are removed from <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> to form the next active set <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> (Lines <a href="#alg1.l11" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>–<a href="#alg1.l13" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We will show that the testing component can require <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\mathinner{\left(k/\epsilon^{2}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\mathinner{\left(k/\epsilon^{2}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> samples. Due to this high testing overhead, it is more efficient to learn each distribution separately whenever <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\ll 1/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\ll 1/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. To address this, <a href="#alg1" title="In 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> incorporates a preliminary check to determine if separate learning yields better complexity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> MDL Meta-Algorithm</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
          Function class <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, distributions <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\dots,P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\dots,P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, total iteration count <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, parameters <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\epsilon^{\prime},\delta,\delta^{\prime}\right)}\in\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\epsilon^{\prime},\delta,\delta^{\prime}\right)}\in\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, condition <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Cond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Cond</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Cond</annotation></semantics></math>, tester function <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Test" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Test</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Test</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Cond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Cond</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Cond</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span> <span id="alg1.l2.5" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Learn each distribution separately.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
             Sample <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{SL}}\mathinner{\left(\eta_{i},\epsilon,\delta/k\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖲𝖫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{SL}}\mathinner{\left(\eta_{i},\epsilon,\delta/k\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
             <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{i}\leftarrow\ERM_{\mathcal{F}}\mathinner{\left(S_{i}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ERM</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{i}\leftarrow\ERM_{\mathcal{F}}\mathinner{\left(S_{i}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
  
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(0)}\leftarrow\mathinner{\left\{P_{1},\dots,P_{k}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(0)}\leftarrow\mathinner{\left\{P_{1},\dots,P_{k}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,\dots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,\dots,T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
            
            
              Sample <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{(t)}\overset{iid}{\sim}\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{(t)}\overset{iid}{\sim}\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S^{(t)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{SL}}\mathinner{\left(\eta,\epsilon^{\prime},\delta^{\prime}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖲𝖫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S^{(t)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{SL}}\mathinner{\left(\eta,\epsilon^{\prime},\delta^{\prime}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
            
            
              <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}\leftarrow\ERM_{\mathcal{F}}\mathinner{\left(S^{(t)}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ERM</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}\leftarrow\ERM_{\mathcal{F}}\mathinner{\left(S^{(t)}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
            
            
              <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t)}\leftarrow\Test\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)},\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>Test</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t)}\leftarrow\Test\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)},\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
            
            
             <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\backslash\mathcal{U}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\backslash\mathcal{U}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
            
            
                <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{i}\leftarrow f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{i}\leftarrow f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
     
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
            
            
          <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{1},\dots,\hat{f}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{1},\dots,\hat{f}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Statistical Learning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under label noise bounded by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, ERM achieves <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-learning with sample complexity</p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{SL}}\mathinner{\left(\eta,\epsilon,\delta\right)}\coloneqq C_{\mathsf{SL}}\cdot\frac{d\log\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}+\log\mathinner{\left(1/\delta\right)}}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖲𝖫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖲𝖫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{SL}}\mathinner{\left(\eta,\epsilon,\delta\right)}\coloneqq C_{\mathsf{SL}}\cdot\frac{d\log\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}+\log\mathinner{\left(1/\delta\right)}}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\mathsf{SL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝖲𝖫</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\mathsf{SL}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a universal constant. We provide further details in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Learning under Massart Noise ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> and refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> for a comprehensive treatment. We leverage this guarantee to determine the sample size for the statistical learning component of <a href="#alg1" title="In 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Specifically, to ensure that the hypothesis <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal with respect to the mixture distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we rely on the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}\subset\mathinner{\left\{P_{1},\dots,P_{k}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}\subset\mathinner{\left\{P_{1},\dots,P_{k}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}}=\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}\right\rvert}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{U}}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}}=\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}\right\rvert}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{U}}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be their uniform mixture. Then, ERM <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}=\ERM_{\mathcal{F}}\mathinner{\left(S\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ERM</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}=\ERM_{\mathcal{F}}\mathinner{\left(S\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> on a sample <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\overset{iid}{\sim}\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\overset{iid}{\sim}\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{SL}}\mathinner{\left(\eta,\epsilon,\delta\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖲𝖫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{SL}}\mathinner{\left(\eta,\epsilon,\delta\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\mathinner{\left(\err\mathinner{\left(\hat{f};\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}}\right)}\leq\epsilon+\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}\right\rvert}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{U}}\eta_{i}^{*}\right)}\geq 1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\mathinner{\left(\err\mathinner{\left(\hat{f};\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}}\right)}\leq\epsilon+\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}\right\rvert}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{U}}\eta_{i}^{*}\right)}\geq 1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Applying this bound to <a href="#alg1" title="In 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we conclude that for any fixed iteration <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathinner{\left[T\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathinner{\left[T\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\mathinner{\left(\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}\right)}\leq\epsilon^{\prime}+\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right\rvert}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}\eta_{i}^{*}\right)}\geq 1-\delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\mathinner{\left(\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};\bar{P}_{\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}\right)}\leq\epsilon^{\prime}+\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right\rvert}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}\eta_{i}^{*}\right)}\geq 1-\delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Conditioned on this high-probability event, we can rewrite the inequality in terms of the average excess error:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right\rvert}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}\mathinner{\left(\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}-\eta_{i}^{*}\right)}\leq\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right\rvert}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}\mathinner{\left(\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}-\eta_{i}^{*}\right)}\leq\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Now, let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="k^{(t)}\coloneqq\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathinner{\left\{i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}-\eta_{i}^{*}\geq 2\epsilon^{\prime}\right\}}\right\rvert}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>err</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ϵ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{(t)}\coloneqq\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathinner{\left\{i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}-\eta_{i}^{*}\geq 2\epsilon^{\prime}\right\}}\right\rvert}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the number of distributions in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> on which <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> performs poorly. Bounding the average excess error from below by the contribution of these <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> distributions yields</p>
<table id="S3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2\epsilon^{\prime}\cdot\frac{k^{(t)}}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right\rvert}}\leq\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right\rvert}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}\mathinner{\left(\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}-\eta_{i}^{*}\right)}\leq\epsilon^{\prime}\Longrightarrow k^{(t)}\leq\frac{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right\rvert}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2\epsilon^{\prime}\cdot\frac{k^{(t)}}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right\rvert}}\leq\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right\rvert}}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}}\mathinner{\left(\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}-\eta_{i}^{*}\right)}\leq\epsilon^{\prime}\Longrightarrow k^{(t)}\leq\frac{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right\rvert}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Consequently, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\epsilon^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\epsilon^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal for at least half of the distributions in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Testing</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the statistical learning step guarantees that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> has low error on at least half of the distributions in the active set <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, the identity of these distributions remains unknown. The following result demonstrates that we can identify them by estimating the empirical error on samples drawn from each distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a distribution over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> be some function, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> be a constant. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\overset{iid}{\sim}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\overset{iid}{\sim}P</annotation></semantics></math> be a sample of size</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}\geq T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\nu,\epsilon,\delta\right)}\coloneqq 16\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{2}{\delta}\right)}\frac{\epsilon+8\nu}{\epsilon^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}\geq T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\nu,\epsilon,\delta\right)}\coloneqq 16\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{2}{\delta}\right)}\frac{\epsilon+8\nu}{\epsilon^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, with probability at least <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the following holds:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{8}+\nu\Longrightarrow\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{2}+\nu\Longrightarrow\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\leq\epsilon+\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{8}+\nu\Longrightarrow\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{2}+\nu\Longrightarrow\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\leq\epsilon+\nu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In essence, this implies that by sampling <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{\epsilon+\nu}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{\epsilon+\nu}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> examples from a distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, we can reliably distinguish whether the error <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-close to a target level <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>(<a href="#S1.Ex3" title="Equation MDL-RCN ‣ RCN. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-RCN</span></a>) Upper Bound</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When our goal is to compete with the noise upper bounds <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the testing problem is simple because the target thresholds are known. Fix a round <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathinner{\left[T\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathinner{\left[T\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall from the statistical learning step that with probability at least <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, for at least half of the distributions in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq 2\epsilon^{\prime}+\eta_{i}^{*}\leq 2\epsilon^{\prime}+\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq 2\epsilon^{\prime}+\eta_{i}^{*}\leq 2\epsilon^{\prime}+\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since each <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is known, we can verify this condition directly. For each distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we draw a sample <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{(t)}_{i}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{(t)}_{i}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\eta_{i},\epsilon,\delta^{\prime\prime}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\eta_{i},\epsilon,\delta^{\prime\prime}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3.2 Testing ‣ 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> together with a union bound, we guarantee that with probability at least <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-k\delta^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-k\delta^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following implications hold for all <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{8}+\eta_{i}\Longrightarrow\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t)}\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{2}+\eta_{i}\Longrightarrow\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon+\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{8}+\eta_{i}\Longrightarrow\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t)}\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{2}+\eta_{i}\Longrightarrow\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon+\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We set <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}=\epsilon/16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}=\epsilon/16</annotation></semantics></math> and define the update rule as follows: eliminate distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if its empirical error satisfies <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t)}\right)}\leq\epsilon/2+\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t)}\right)}\leq\epsilon/2+\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. This strategy guarantees two key properties:</p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Progress)</span> At least half of the distributions in <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> (specifically those with <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/8+\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/8+\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) will satisfy the empirical condition and be eliminated.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Correctness)</span> Any eliminated distribution is guaranteed to satisfy <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon+\eta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon+\eta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Thus, in each round <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we successfully learn and remove at least half of the active distributions with probability <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta^{\prime}-k\delta^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta^{\prime}-k\delta^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Appropriately tuning the parameters yields the procedure described in <a href="#alg2" title="In 3.3 () Upper Bound ‣ 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> <math id="alg2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\RCNTest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>RCN-Test</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\RCNTest</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Decision <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, distribution set <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
              Sample <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}^{(t)}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}^{(t)}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="alg2.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\eta_{i},\epsilon,\frac{\delta}{2kT}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\eta_{i},\epsilon,\frac{\delta}{2kT}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t)}\leftarrow\mathinner{\left\{i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t)}\right)}&gt;\epsilon/2+\eta_{i}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t)}\leftarrow\mathinner{\left\{i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t)}\right)}&gt;\epsilon/2+\eta_{i}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>((<a href="#S1.Ex3" title="Equation MDL-RCN ‣ RCN. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-RCN</span></a>) upper bound)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\left\lceil\log_{2}k\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\left\lceil\log_{2}k\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}=\frac{\epsilon}{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>16</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}=\frac{\epsilon}{16}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{\prime}=\frac{\delta}{2T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{\prime}=\frac{\delta}{2T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Test=\RCNTest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Test</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>RCN-Test</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Test=\RCNTest</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, define the condition <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Cond=\mathinner{\left\{T_{\text{joint}}&gt;T_{\text{sep}}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cond</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">joint</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">sep</mtext></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Cond=\mathinner{\left\{T_{\text{joint}}&gt;T_{\text{sep}}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{joint}}\coloneqq\log^{2}\mathinner{\left(\frac{k}{\delta}\right)}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d\log\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{\epsilon+\eta_{i}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}\quad\text{and}\quad T_{\mathsf{sep}}\coloneqq\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{d\log\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}+\log\mathinner{\left(k/\delta\right)}}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta_{i}\right)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗂𝗇𝗍</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝗌𝖾𝗉</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{joint}}\coloneqq\log^{2}\mathinner{\left(\frac{k}{\delta}\right)}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d\log\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{\epsilon+\eta_{i}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}\quad\text{and}\quad T_{\mathsf{sep}}\coloneqq\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{d\log\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}+\log\mathinner{\left(k/\delta\right)}}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta_{i}\right)}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, <a href="#alg1" title="In 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> solves (<a href="#S1.Ex3" title="Equation MDL-RCN ‣ RCN. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-RCN</span></a>) with sample complexity</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{RCN}}\mathinner{\left(\eta_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\epsilon,\delta\right)}=O\mathinner{\left(\min\mathinner{\left\{T_{\mathsf{joint}},T_{\mathsf{sep}}\right\}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝖱𝖢𝖭</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝗃𝗈𝗂𝗇𝗍</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝗌𝖾𝗉</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{RCN}}\mathinner{\left(\eta_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\epsilon,\delta\right)}=O\mathinner{\left(\min\mathinner{\left\{T_{\mathsf{joint}},T_{\mathsf{sep}}\right\}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the special case where the noise upper bound is uniform across distributions—that is, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}=\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}=\eta</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>—the sample complexity bound from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 (() upper bound). ‣ 3.3 () Upper Bound ‣ 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> simplifies (ignoring logarithmic factors) to</p>
<table id="S3.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\epsilon+\eta\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{dk}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}\right\}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\epsilon+\eta\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{dk}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}\right\}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>(<a href="#S1.Ex4" title="Equation MDL-MM ‣ Minimax. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-MM</span></a>) Upper Bound</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When our goal is to compete with the <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unknown</span> maximum noise rate <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}=\max_{i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}}\eta_{i}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}=\max_{i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}}\eta_{i}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the previous testing strategy is inapplicable because we lack the explicit thresholds required for <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\RCNTest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>RCN-Test</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\RCNTest</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fix a round <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math>. The statistical learning component guarantees that with probability at least <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, at least half of the distributions <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</p>
<table id="S3.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq 2\epsilon^{\prime}+\eta_{i}^{*}\leq 2\epsilon^{\prime}+\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq 2\epsilon^{\prime}+\eta_{i}^{*}\leq 2\epsilon^{\prime}+\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> was known, we could simply set <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}=\epsilon/16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}=\epsilon/16</annotation></semantics></math> and invoke <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\RCNTest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>RCN-Test</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\RCNTest</annotation></semantics></math> using the uniform threshold <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon/2+\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon/2+\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown, we proceed by guessing its value in small increments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> denote a candidate guess for <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. We set <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}=\epsilon/32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}=\epsilon/32</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring that with probability at least <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, half of the active distributions satisfy <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/16+\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/16+\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3.2 Testing ‣ 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> guarantees that by drawing a sample <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}^{(t)}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}^{(t)}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\nu,\epsilon/2,\delta^{\prime\prime}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\nu,\epsilon/2,\delta^{\prime\prime}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following holds with probability <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-k\delta^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-k\delta^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>: for every <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{16}+\nu\Longrightarrow\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t)}\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{4}+\nu\Longrightarrow\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{2}+\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>16</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{16}+\nu\Longrightarrow\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t)}\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{4}+\nu\Longrightarrow\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{2}+\nu</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Based on this, if we remove all <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., output <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) such that <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t)}\right)}\leq\epsilon/4+\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t)}\right)}\leq\epsilon/4+\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, we ensure the following:</p>
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Progress)</span> Any distribution with true error <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/16+\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/16+\nu</annotation></semantics></math> will be removed.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Correctness)</span> Any removed distribution satisfies <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/2+\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/2+\nu</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">We begin with the candidate guess <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Following the idea above, we draw samples <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}^{(t,1)}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}^{(t,1)}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t,1)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(0,\epsilon/2,\delta^{\prime\prime}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t,1)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(0,\epsilon/2,\delta^{\prime\prime}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> for each active distribution and eliminate those satisfying <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t,1)}\right)}\leq\epsilon/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t,1)}\right)}\leq\epsilon/4</annotation></semantics></math>. If this step results in the removal of at least half of <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we terminate the current round <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Crucially, the Correctness property ensures that any removed distribution satisfies <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/2\leq\epsilon+\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/2\leq\epsilon+\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, guaranteeing that we only output good hypotheses. Furthermore, if the true noise rate is indeed <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the Progress property guarantees that this round terminates. By union bounding with the ERM guarantee, all of the above holds with probability <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta^{\prime}-k\delta^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta^{\prime}-k\delta^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If the first check fails to eliminate half of the distributions, we proceed with the updated guess <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Rather than discarding the previous data, we augment the existing samples: for each remaining distribution <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we draw a fresh sample <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t,2)}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t,2)}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t,2)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon/2,\epsilon/2,\delta^{\prime\prime}\right)}-\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t,1)}\right\rvert}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t,2)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon/2,\epsilon/2,\delta^{\prime\prime}\right)}-\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t,1)}\right\rvert}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then define <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}^{(t,2)}=S_{i}^{(t,1)}\cup\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t,2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}^{(t,2)}=S_{i}^{(t,1)}\cup\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t,2)}</annotation></semantics></math> as the aggregated sample set, which now satisfies the required size <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t,2)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon/2,\epsilon/2,\delta^{\prime\prime}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t,2)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon/2,\epsilon/2,\delta^{\prime\prime}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We eliminate any distribution satisfying <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t,2)}\right)}\leq\epsilon/4+\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t,2)}\right)}\leq\epsilon/4+\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>, terminating the round if at least half of <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> has been removed. Observe that this threshold guarantees the removal of any <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/16+\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/16+\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>, due to Progress. Consequently, if the true noise rate lies in the range <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}\in(0,\epsilon/2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}\in(0,\epsilon/2]</annotation></semantics></math>, the round terminates. Furthermore, Correctness ensures that any removed distribution satisfies <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/2+\epsilon/2\leq\epsilon+\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/2+\epsilon/2\leq\epsilon+\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking a union bound over both testing sub-rounds, all of the above holds with probability at least <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta^{\prime}-2k\delta^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta^{\prime}-2k\delta^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We continue iteratively, testing the hypotheses that <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> lies within consecutive intervals <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left\{0\right\}},(0,\epsilon/2],(\epsilon/2,\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left\{0\right\}},(0,\epsilon/2],(\epsilon/2,\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math>, and so on. Suppose that the true noise rate falls in the interval <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}\in(\mathinner{\left(m-1\right)}\epsilon/2,m\epsilon/2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}\in(\mathinner{\left(m-1\right)}\epsilon/2,m\epsilon/2]</annotation></semantics></math> and the procedure has not yet terminated in the first <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> iterations. Then in the <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(m+1\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(m+1\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>th iteration, we set the guess <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=m\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=m\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>. By assumption, we know that <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq 2\eta^{*}/\epsilon+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq 2\eta^{*}/\epsilon+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}\leq m\epsilon/2\leq\eta^{*}+\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}\leq m\epsilon/2\leq\eta^{*}+\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>. For each remaining distribution <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t,m+1)}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t,m+1)}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the aggregated sample <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}^{(t,m+1)}=S_{i}^{(t,m)}\cup\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t,m+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}^{(t,m+1)}=S_{i}^{(t,m)}\cup\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t,m+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> has size</p>
<table id="S3.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t,m+1)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(m\epsilon/2,\epsilon/2,\delta^{\prime\prime}\right)}=32\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{2}{\delta^{\prime\prime}}\right)}\frac{\epsilon+16m\epsilon/2}{\epsilon^{2}}\leq 32\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{2}{\delta^{\prime\prime}}\right)}\frac{9\epsilon+16\eta^{*}}{\epsilon^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t,m+1)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(m\epsilon/2,\epsilon/2,\delta^{\prime\prime}\right)}=32\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{2}{\delta^{\prime\prime}}\right)}\frac{\epsilon+16m\epsilon/2}{\epsilon^{2}}\leq 32\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{2}{\delta^{\prime\prime}}\right)}\frac{9\epsilon+16\eta^{*}}{\epsilon^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.2" class="ltx_p">We then remove any distribution satisfying <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t,m+1)}\right)}\leq\epsilon/4+m\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t,m+1)}\right)}\leq\epsilon/4+m\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Once again, the known properties yield the following:</p>
<ul id="S3.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Progress:</span> We are guaranteed to remove any <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/16+m\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/16+m\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Since we know that <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/16+\eta^{*}\leq\epsilon/16+m\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon/16+\eta^{*}\leq\epsilon/16+m\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math> for at least half of <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, the procedure is guaranteed to terminate at this step.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correctness:</span> Any distribution <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> removed in this iteration satisfies</p>
<table id="S3.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{2}+\frac{m\epsilon}{2}=\epsilon+\frac{\mathinner{\left(m-1\right)}\epsilon}{2}\leq\epsilon+\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\frac{\epsilon}{2}+\frac{m\epsilon}{2}=\epsilon+\frac{\mathinner{\left(m-1\right)}\epsilon}{2}\leq\epsilon+\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.3" class="ltx_p">Consequently, the testing phase for round <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> terminates in at most <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+1\leq 2\eta^{*}/\epsilon+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+1\leq 2\eta^{*}/\epsilon+2</annotation></semantics></math> iterations. Crucially, every eliminated distribution <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the target guarantee <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon+\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};P_{i}\right)}\leq\epsilon+\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. By a union bound, this result holds with probability at least</p>
<table id="S3.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1-\delta^{\prime}-\mathinner{\left(m+1\right)}k\delta^{\prime\prime}\geq 1-\delta^{\prime}-2\mathinner{\left(\eta^{*}/\epsilon+1\right)}k\delta^{\prime\prime}\geq 1-\delta^{\prime}-2\mathinner{\left(\eta/\epsilon+1\right)}k\delta^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1-\delta^{\prime}-\mathinner{\left(m+1\right)}k\delta^{\prime\prime}\geq 1-\delta^{\prime}-2\mathinner{\left(\eta^{*}/\epsilon+1\right)}k\delta^{\prime\prime}\geq 1-\delta^{\prime}-2\mathinner{\left(\eta/\epsilon+1\right)}k\delta^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.4" class="ltx_p">The full procedure is detailed in <a href="#alg3" title="In 3.4 () Upper Bound ‣ 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg3" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span> <math id="alg3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\MMTest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>MM-Test</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\MMTest</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
<div id="alg3.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg3.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Decision <math id="alg3.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, distribution set <math id="alg3.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg3.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t)}\leftarrow\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t)}\leftarrow\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg3.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg3.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg3.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg3.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}^{(t)}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}^{(t)}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg3.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <math id="alg3.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t)}\right\rvert}&gt;\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right\rvert}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t)}\right\rvert}&gt;\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\mathcal{U}^{(t-1)}\right\rvert}/2</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg3.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg3.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg3.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathcal{U}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg3.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
               Sample <math id="alg3.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t)}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t)}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="alg3.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\nu,\epsilon/2,\frac{\delta}{4\mathinner{\left(\eta/\epsilon+1\right)}kT}\right)}-\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t)}\right\rvert}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t)}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{T}}\mathinner{\left(\nu,\epsilon/2,\frac{\delta}{4\mathinner{\left(\eta/\epsilon+1\right)}kT}\right)}-\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{(t)}\right\rvert}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg3.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}^{(t)}\leftarrow S_{i}^{(t)}\cup\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}^{(t)}\leftarrow S_{i}^{(t)}\cup\tilde{S}_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg3.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg3.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t)}\right)}\leq\epsilon/4+\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{(t)};S_{i}^{(t)}\right)}\leq\epsilon/4+\nu</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg3.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg3.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
                 Remove <math id="alg3.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg3.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
     
</div>
<div id="alg3.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg3.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\leftarrow\nu+\epsilon/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\leftarrow\nu+\epsilon/2</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg3.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg3.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg3.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>((<a href="#S1.Ex4" title="Equation MDL-MM ‣ Minimax. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-MM</span></a>) upper bound)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\left\lceil\log_{2}k\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\left\lceil\log_{2}k\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{\prime}=\frac{\epsilon}{32}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>32</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{\prime}=\frac{\epsilon}{32}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{\prime}=\frac{\delta}{2T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{\prime}=\frac{\delta}{2T}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Cond=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cond</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Cond=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Test=\MMTest" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Test</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>MM-Test</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Test=\MMTest</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <a href="#alg1" title="In 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> solves (<a href="#S1.Ex4" title="Equation MDL-MM ‣ Minimax. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-MM</span></a>) with sample complexity</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{MM}}\mathinner{\left(\eta_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k}^{*},\eta_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\epsilon,\delta\right)}=O\mathinner{\left(\log^{2}\mathinner{\left(\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\eta/\epsilon+1\right)}}{\delta}\right)}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d\log\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\epsilon+\eta^{*}\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝖬𝖬</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{MM}}\mathinner{\left(\eta_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k}^{*},\eta_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\epsilon,\delta\right)}=O\mathinner{\left(\log^{2}\mathinner{\left(\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\eta/\epsilon+1\right)}}{\delta}\right)}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d\log\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\epsilon+\eta^{*}\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremarkx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremarkx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmremarkx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>(Condition for separate learning)<span id="Thmremarkx1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremarkx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremarkx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our algorithm for (<a href="#S1.Ex4" title="Equation MDL-MM ‣ Minimax. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-MM</span></a>), we do not explicitly enforce a switching condition for separate learning because the bound in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (() upper bound). ‣ 3.4 () Upper Bound ‣ 3 MDL Upper Bounds ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> depends on the unknown <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, if we use the upper bound <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> as a proxy for <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and define</p>
<table id="S3.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Cond=\mathinner{\left\{\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\epsilon+\eta\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}}&gt;\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta_{i}\right)}}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cond</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&gt;</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Cond=\mathinner{\left\{\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\epsilon+\eta\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}}&gt;\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta_{i}\right)}}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmremarkx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">then we achieve a sample complexity of <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta_{i}\right)}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta_{i}\right)}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Cond" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Cond</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Cond</annotation></semantics></math> holds, and <math id="Thmremarkx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\epsilon+\eta^{*}\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\epsilon+\eta^{*}\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Structured Hypothesis Testing</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a precursor to establishing the MDL lower bound, we first investigate a related problem. Throughout this section, we fix the noise level to <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=1/4</annotation></semantics></math> to focus our analysis on the other problem parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a hypothesis class with VC-dimension <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a distribution over <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the Bayes classifier <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfies the noise condition <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\middle|X=x\right)}=1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\middle|X=x\right)}=1/4</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. This corresponds to the standard RCN setting with fixed, known noise. We are given a query function <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> (not necessarily in <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>) and tasked with determining whether <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal with respect to <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, the algorithm must draw i.i.d. samples from <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and output a decision <math id="S4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{D}\in\mathinner{\left\{\mathsf{YES},\mathsf{NO}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖣</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>𝖸𝖤𝖲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖭𝖮</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{D}\in\mathinner{\left\{\mathsf{YES},\mathsf{NO}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies the following conditions with probability at least <math id="S4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.Ex32X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex32X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{If $\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\leq 1/4+\epsilon/12$, output $\mathsf{D}=\mathsf{YES}$.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>If </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mtext>, output </mtext><mrow><mi>𝖣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖸𝖤𝖲</mi></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{If $\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\leq 1/4+\epsilon/12$, output $\mathsf{D}=\mathsf{YES}$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(SHT)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.Ex32Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex32Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{If $\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\geq 1/4+\epsilon$, output $\mathsf{D}=\mathsf{NO}$.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>If </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext>, output </mtext><mrow><mi>𝖣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖭𝖮</mi></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{If $\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\geq 1/4+\epsilon$, output $\mathsf{D}=\mathsf{NO}$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">We refer to this problem as <span id="S4.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Structured Hypothesis Testing (SHT)</span>. We say that an algorithm <math id="S4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> for (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) has sample complexity <math id="S4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}^{2}\to\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}^{2}\to\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> if, for any valid instance <math id="S4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(\mathcal{F},P,f,\epsilon,\delta\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(\mathcal{F},P,f,\epsilon,\delta\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, it satisfies the conditions above using at most <math id="S4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> samples.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>(<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) Upper Bounds</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present two distinct strategies for solving the (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) problem.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Agnostic Testing.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the goal is to test whether <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\mathinner{\left(f\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\mathinner{\left(f\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/4</annotation></semantics></math>, we can simply disregard the supervised nature of the data and work directly with the empirical errors <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(\mathbb{I}\mathinner{\left\{f\mathinner{\left(X_{t}\right)}\neq Y_{t}\right\}}\right)}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(\mathbb{I}\mathinner{\left\{f\mathinner{\left(X_{t}\right)}\neq Y_{t}\right\}}\right)}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The problem then reduces to distinguishing between two Bernoulli biases separated by a gap of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta\mathinner{\left(\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta\mathinner{\left(\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which requires a sample complexity of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta\mathinner{\left(\log\mathinner{\left(1/\delta\right)}/\epsilon^{2}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta\mathinner{\left(\log\mathinner{\left(1/\delta\right)}/\epsilon^{2}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Testing via empirical errors)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed function, and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\overset{iid}{\sim}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\overset{iid}{\sim}P</annotation></semantics></math> be a sample of size</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}\geq T_{\mathsf{C}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}\coloneqq\frac{72\log\mathinner{\left(2/\delta\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖢</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>72</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}\geq T_{\mathsf{C}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}\coloneqq\frac{72\log\mathinner{\left(2/\delta\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, with probability at least <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the following are true:</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}\leq 1/4+\epsilon/6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}\leq 1/4+\epsilon/6</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\leq 1/4+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\leq 1/4+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}&gt;1/4+\epsilon/6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}&gt;1/4+\epsilon/6</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}&gt;1/4+\epsilon/12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}&gt;1/4+\epsilon/12</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Learning-Augmented Testing.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An alternative approach is to first learn a reference hypothesis <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(\hat{f};P\right)}\leq 1/4+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(\hat{f};P\right)}\leq 1/4+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> with high probability. This can be achieved by performing ERM on a sample of size <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\mathinner{\left(d\log\mathinner{\left(1/\delta\right)}/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\mathinner{\left(d\log\mathinner{\left(1/\delta\right)}/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. With <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}</annotation></semantics></math> in hand, we can draw an additional sample to efficiently test the quality of a candidate function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. In the following lemma, we establish the validity of this approach for a general noise level <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, as this broader result will be instrumental in later sections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(From learning to testing)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a distribution over <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y|X=x\right)}\leq\eta\quad\forall x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y|X=x\right)}\leq\eta\quad\forall x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}\coloneqq P\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}\coloneqq P\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the optimal error. Suppose we have access to a reference hypothesis <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> (possibly random) that satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\mathinner{\left(\err\mathinner{\left(\hat{f};P\right)}\leq\eta^{*}+\frac{\epsilon}{48}\right)}\geq 1-\frac{\delta}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>48</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\mathinner{\left(\err\mathinner{\left(\hat{f};P\right)}\leq\eta^{*}+\frac{\epsilon}{48}\right)}\geq 1-\frac{\delta}{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed candidate function, and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\overset{iid}{\sim}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\overset{iid}{\sim}P</annotation></semantics></math> be a sample drawn independently of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, of size</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx36" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}\geq T_{\mathsf{L}}\mathinner{\left(\eta,\epsilon,\delta\right)}\coloneqq\frac{96}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{6}{\delta}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>96</mn><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}\geq T_{\mathsf{L}}\mathinner{\left(\eta,\epsilon,\delta\right)}\coloneqq\frac{96}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{6}{\delta}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, with probability at least <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the following are true:</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(\hat{f};S\right)}\right\rvert}\leq\epsilon/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(\hat{f};S\right)}\right\rvert}\leq\epsilon/3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\leq\eta^{*}+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}\leq\eta^{*}+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(\hat{f};S\right)}\right\rvert}&gt;\epsilon/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(\hat{f};S\right)}\right\rvert}&gt;\epsilon/3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}&gt;\eta^{*}+\epsilon/12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}&gt;\eta^{*}+\epsilon/12</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>), we apply <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 4.2 (From learning to testing). ‣ Learning-Augmented Testing. ‣ 4.1 () Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\eta^{*}=1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\eta^{*}=1/4</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields a sample size of</p>
<table id="S4.EGx37" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{L}}\mathinner{\left(\frac{1}{4},\epsilon,\delta\right)}=\frac{192}{\epsilon}\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{6}{\delta}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>192</mn><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{L}}\mathinner{\left(\frac{1}{4},\epsilon,\delta\right)}=\frac{192}{\epsilon}\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{6}{\delta}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">By combining the agnostic and learning-augmented strategies into a single procedure (<a href="#alg4" title="In Learning-Augmented Testing. ‣ 4.1 () Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), we obtain the following guarantee.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg4" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 4</span> </span> SHT</figcaption>
<div id="alg4.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
