<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>((<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) upper bound)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#alg4" title="In Learning-Augmented Testing. ‣ 4.1 () Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> solves the (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) problem with sample complexity</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx38" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{SHT}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}=O\mathinner{\left(\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖲𝖧𝖳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{SHT}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}=O\mathinner{\left(\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This bound highlights a natural trade-off: in the low-precision regime where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\gg 1/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≫</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\gg 1/d</annotation></semantics></math>, the agnostic testing approach (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\epsilon^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\epsilon^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) is superior. However, in the high-precision regime where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\ll 1/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\ll 1/d</annotation></semantics></math>, exploiting the supervised structure of the data via the learning-augmented approach (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>) yields an improvement.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>(<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) Lower Bound</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that under the RCN assumption, ERM requires only <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> samples, a significant improvement over the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon^{2}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon^{2}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> complexity of the fully agnostic setting. It is natural to ask whether the testing problem (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) can similarly be solved with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The fast rate for ERM relies on a critical variance bound: for any distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the RCN condition under constant noise, we have that (see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Learning under Massart Noise ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>)</p>
<table id="S4.EGx39" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Var_{P}\mathinner{\left(\mathbb{I}\mathinner{\left\{f\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\right\}}-\mathbb{I}\mathinner{\left\{f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\right\}}\right)}\lesssim\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}-\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Var</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Var_{P}\mathinner{\left(\mathbb{I}\mathinner{\left\{f\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\right\}}-\mathbb{I}\mathinner{\left\{f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\right\}}\right)}\lesssim\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}-\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">That is, we can control the variance when we <span id="S4.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">offset</span> by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Bernstein’s inequality then ensures that with high probability,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx40" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}-\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P\right)}-\mathinner{\left(\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{*};S\right)}\right)}\right\rvert}\lesssim\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}}+\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}-\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}-\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P\right)}-\mathinner{\left(\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{*};S\right)}\right)}\right\rvert}\lesssim\frac{1}{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}}+\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}-\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">for a sample <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\overset{iid}{\sim}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\overset{iid}{\sim}P</annotation></semantics></math>. If we had access to the quantity <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{*};S\right)}\right\rvert}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{*};S\right)}\right\rvert}</annotation></semantics></math>, we could perform the test efficiently using the reasoning of <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 4.2 (From learning to testing). ‣ Learning-Augmented Testing. ‣ 4.1 () Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. In fact, we showed that having access to an <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal hypothesis (a proxy for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>) allows us to test with just <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> additional samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, a fundamental obstacle remains: we do not know <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Although we know its population error is <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P\right)}=1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P\right)}=1/4</annotation></semantics></math>, we cannot readily use its empirical error <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{*};S\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{*};S\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> as a reference point because the concentration of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{*};S\right)}\right\rvert}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(f^{*};S\right)}\right\rvert}</annotation></semantics></math> is slow, scaling with <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(1/\sqrt{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(1/\sqrt{\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S\right\rvert}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, the ERM sample size of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> ensures uniform convergence of the excess risks (the offset counterparts), but not of the absolute errors <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}\right\rvert}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\err\mathinner{\left(f;P\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(f;S\right)}\right\rvert}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the following result, we prove that the trade-off achieved by <a href="#alg4" title="In Learning-Augmented Testing. ‣ 4.1 () Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is optimal. Specifically, we show that the sample complexity is lower-bounded by the minimum of the agnostic rate and the learning-augmented rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>((<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) lower bound)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leq 1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leq 1/4</annotation></semantics></math>. Any algorithm <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> that solves (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) requires a sample size of</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx41" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}\geq\frac{3}{16}\log\mathinner{\left(1+\log 2\right)}\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}\geq\frac{3}{16}\log\mathinner{\left(1+\log 2\right)}\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch of <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (() lower bound). ‣ 4.2 () Lower Bound ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We will show the bound <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq\frac{1}{2\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq\frac{1}{2\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;\frac{1}{2\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;\frac{1}{2\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can simply choose a shattered set of size <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the lower bound <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon^{2}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon^{2}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Hence, assume that <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq\frac{1}{2\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq\frac{1}{2\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> and suppose that <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\llbracket d^{2}\rrbracket" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\llbracket d^{2}\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}(0)=1-2d\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}(0)=1-2d\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}(x)=2\epsilon/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}(x)=2\epsilon/d</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathinner{\left[d^{2}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathinner{\left[d^{2}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the following hypotheses:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex42" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex42X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex42X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{$H_{0}$: $f^{*}=f_{0}$, where $f_{0}\coloneqq x\mapsto 0$ is the constant zero function.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext>: </mtext><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mtext>, where </mtext><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mtext> is the constant zero function.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{$H_{0}$: $f^{*}=f_{0}$, where $f_{0}\coloneqq x\mapsto 0$ is the constant zero function.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex42Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex42Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{$H_{1}$: $f^{*}$ equals $1$ precisely on a random subset $R\subset\mathinner{\left[d^{2}\right]}$ of size $\mathinner{\!\left\lvert R\right\rvert}=d$.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mtext>: </mtext><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mtext> equals </mtext><mn>1</mn><mtext> precisely on a random subset </mtext><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mtext> of size </mtext><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{$H_{1}$: $f^{*}$ equals $1$ precisely on a random subset $R\subset\mathinner{\left[d^{2}\right]}$ of size $\mathinner{\!\left\lvert R\right\rvert}=d$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Note that we never need to shatter a set of size larger than <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Under <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx42" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\mathinner{\left(Y=1\right)}=\mathinner{\left(1-2d\epsilon\right)}\cdot\frac{1}{4}+\mathinner{\left(d^{2}-d\right)}\cdot\frac{2\epsilon}{d}\cdot\frac{1}{4}+d\cdot\frac{2\epsilon}{d}\cdot\frac{3}{4}=\frac{1}{4}+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\mathinner{\left(Y=1\right)}=\mathinner{\left(1-2d\epsilon\right)}\cdot\frac{1}{4}+\mathinner{\left(d^{2}-d\right)}\cdot\frac{2\epsilon}{d}\cdot\frac{1}{4}+d\cdot\frac{2\epsilon}{d}\cdot\frac{3}{4}=\frac{1}{4}+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">In particular, <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has errors <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/4</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/4+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/4+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that an (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) learner should be able to distinguish both hypotheses. Using Ingster’s method, we can show that testing <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> under a uniform mixture over subsets <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> requires <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> samples.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Combining <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (() upper bound). ‣ Learning-Augmented Testing. ‣ 4.1 () Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (() lower bound). ‣ 4.2 () Lower Bound ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, we conclude that the agnostic and learning-augmented strategies fundamentally characterize the difficulty of the (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) problem, yielding a sample complexity of</p>
<table id="S4.EGx43" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Multi-Distribution SHT</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now extend the (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) framework to the multi-distribution setting. Let <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\dots,P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\dots,P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be distributions over <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> that all satisfy the RCN condition</p>
<table id="S4.EGx44" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{i}\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\middle|X=x\right)}=\frac{1}{4}\quad\forall x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{i}\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y\middle|X=x\right)}=\frac{1}{4}\quad\forall x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with respect to a <span id="S4.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">shared</span> unknown <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. We are given candidate functions <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},\dots,f_{k}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},\dots,f_{k}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> (not necessarily in <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>), and our objective is to determine whether <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\err\mathinner{\left(f_{i};P_{i}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\err\mathinner{\left(f_{i};P_{i}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-optimal for every <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">More precisely, the algorithm draws a total of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> samples (possibly adaptively) from the distributions and outputs a decision vector <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(\mathsf{D}_{1},\dots,\mathsf{D}_{k}\right)}\in\mathinner{\left\{\mathsf{YES},\mathsf{NO}\right\}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>𝖸𝖤𝖲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝖭𝖮</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(\mathsf{D}_{1},\dots,\mathsf{D}_{k}\right)}\in\mathinner{\left\{\mathsf{YES},\mathsf{NO}\right\}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. We require that for each <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability at least <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the following condition holds:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex46" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.Ex46X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex46X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{If $\err\mathinner{\left(f_{i};P_{i}\right)}\leq 1/4+\epsilon/12$, output $\mathsf{D}_{i}=\mathsf{YES}$.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>If </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mtext>, output </mtext><mrow><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖸𝖤𝖲</mi></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{If $\err\mathinner{\left(f_{i};P_{i}\right)}\leq 1/4+\epsilon/12$, output $\mathsf{D}_{i}=\mathsf{YES}$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(MSHT)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.Ex46Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex46Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{If $\err\mathinner{\left(f_{i};P_{i}\right)}\geq 1/4+\epsilon$, output $\mathsf{D}_{i}=\mathsf{NO}$.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>If </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext>, output </mtext><mrow><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖭𝖮</mi></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{If $\err\mathinner{\left(f_{i};P_{i}\right)}\geq 1/4+\epsilon$, output $\mathsf{D}_{i}=\mathsf{NO}$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">We say that an algorithm <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> for (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) has sample complexity <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}^{2}\to\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}^{2}\to\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> if it satisfies this guarantee for any valid instance <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(\mathcal{F},P_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},f_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\epsilon,\delta\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(\mathcal{F},P_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},f_{1\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}k},\epsilon,\delta\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> using at most <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> total samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremarkx2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremarkx2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark</span></span><span id="Thmremarkx2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>((<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) high-probability guarantee)<span id="Thmremarkx2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremarkx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremarkx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that we only require that each <math id="Thmremarkx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{D}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{D}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> succeeds with high-probability. That is, we do not require <span id="Thmremarkx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span> decisions to be correct simultaneously. The reason for this formulation is to avoid a <math id="Thmremarkx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log k</annotation></semantics></math> factor when reducing to (<a href="#S1.Ex3" title="Equation MDL-RCN ‣ RCN. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-RCN</span></a>). Of course, one can easily ensure simultaneous success by replacing <math id="Thmremarkx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmremarkx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta/k</annotation></semantics></math> and taking a union bound over <math id="Thmremarkx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="alg5" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 5</span> </span> MSHT</figcaption>
<div id="alg5.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg5.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Function class <math id="alg5.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, distributions <math id="alg5.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\dots,P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\dots,P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, functions <math id="alg5.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},\dots,f_{k}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},\dots,f_{k}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, parameters <math id="alg5.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}\in\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}\in\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg5.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg5.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg5.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg5.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg5.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg5.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg5.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
               Sample <math id="alg5.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="alg5.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{C}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖢</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{C}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg5.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg5.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg5.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emperr\mathinner{\left(f_{i};S_{i}\right)}\leq 1/4+\epsilon/6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emperr\mathinner{\left(f_{i};S_{i}\right)}\leq 1/4+\epsilon/6</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg5.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
                 <math id="alg5.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{D}_{i}\leftarrow\mathsf{YES}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝖸𝖤𝖲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{D}_{i}\leftarrow\mathsf{YES}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg5.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg5.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="alg5.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
                 <math id="alg5.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{D}_{i}\leftarrow\mathsf{NO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝖭𝖮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{D}_{i}\leftarrow\mathsf{NO}</annotation></semantics></math>.
     
</div>
<div id="alg5.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg5.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="alg5.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg5.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg5.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg5.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
               Sample <math id="alg5.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="alg5.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{SL}}\mathinner{\left(1/4,\epsilon/48,\delta/3\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖲𝖫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{SL}}\mathinner{\left(1/4,\epsilon/48,\delta/3\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg5.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg5.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{i}\leftarrow\ERM_{\mathcal{F}}\mathinner{\left(S_{i}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ERM</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{i}\leftarrow\ERM_{\mathcal{F}}\mathinner{\left(S_{i}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg5.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
               Sample <math id="alg5.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}^{\prime}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}^{\prime}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="alg5.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{\prime}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{L}}\mathinner{\left(1/4,\epsilon,\delta\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}^{\prime}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{L}}\mathinner{\left(1/4,\epsilon,\delta\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg5.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg5.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg5.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\emperr\mathinner{\left(f_{i};S_{i}^{\prime}\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(\hat{f}_{i};S_{i}^{\prime}\right)}\right\rvert}\leq\epsilon/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\emperr\mathinner{\left(f_{i};S_{i}^{\prime}\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(\hat{f}_{i};S_{i}^{\prime}\right)}\right\rvert}\leq\epsilon/3</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg5.l14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
                 <math id="alg5.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{D}_{i}\leftarrow\mathsf{YES}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝖸𝖤𝖲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{D}_{i}\leftarrow\mathsf{YES}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg5.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg5.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="alg5.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
              
              
                 <math id="alg5.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{D}_{i}\leftarrow\mathsf{NO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝖭𝖮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{D}_{i}\leftarrow\mathsf{NO}</annotation></semantics></math>.
     
</div>
<div id="alg5.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg5.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
              
              
            Decision vector <math id="alg5.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(\mathsf{D}_{1},\dots,\mathsf{D}_{k}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(\mathsf{D}_{1},\dots,\mathsf{D}_{k}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To solve the (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) problem, we apply the strategy of <a href="#alg4" title="In Learning-Augmented Testing. ‣ 4.1 () Upper Bounds ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> to each distribution individually. This procedure is detailed in <a href="#alg5" title="In 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, and its sample complexity is established in <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5 (() upper bound). ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>((<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) upper bound)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#alg5" title="In 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> solves the (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) problem with sample complexity</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx45" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{MSHT}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}=O\mathinner{\left(k\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖬𝖲𝖧𝖳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathsf{MSHT}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}=O\mathinner{\left(k\log\mathinner{\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Testing each distribution separately results in a <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-fold increase in sample complexity relative to the single-distribution setting. We now show that this linear scaling is unavoidable. In particular, the following result establishes that in the worst case, no algorithm can solve (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) without essentially performing independent tests.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Multi-Distribution SHT lower bound)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leq 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leq 0.01</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 8\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 8\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Any algorithm <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> that solves (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) requires a sample size of</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx46" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}\geq\frac{0.015k}{4}\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.015</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}\geq\frac{0.015k}{4}\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch of <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 4.6 (Multi-Distribution SHT lower bound). ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Again, we focus on the setting where <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq\frac{1}{2\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq\frac{1}{2\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> and aim to show the lower bound <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\mathinner{\left(kd/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\mathinner{\left(kd/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. This immediately implies the <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\mathinner{\left(k/\epsilon^{2}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\mathinner{\left(k/\epsilon^{2}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> bound when <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;\frac{1}{2\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;\frac{1}{2\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">In essence, we will construct distributions whose <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>-marginals will be supported on consecutive disjoint sets of size <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{2}+1</annotation></semantics></math>, each with the same weights as in the (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) lower bound. We then consider the following testing problem:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex49" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex49X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex49X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{$H_{0}$: $f^{*}=f_{0}$.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext>: </mtext><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{$H_{0}$: $f^{*}=f_{0}$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex49Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex49Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{$H_{1}$: $f^{*}$ equals $1$ precisely on a size-$d$ subset on the support of one of the distributions.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mtext>: </mtext><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mtext> equals </mtext><mn>1</mn><mtext> precisely on a size-</mtext><mi>d</mi><mtext> subset on the support of one of the distributions.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{$H_{1}$: $f^{*}$ equals $1$ precisely on a size-$d$ subset on the support of one of the distributions.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">which still only requires shattering a set of size <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. We then show that the learner must sample <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> times from each distribution by constructing an (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) algorithm that simulates the (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) one.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Combining the upper and lower bounds, we conclude that the sample complexity of the (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) problem is</p>
<table id="S4.EGx47" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\Theta}\mathinner{\left(k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>MDL Lower Bound</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As in <a href="#S4" title="4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we establish the lower bound under RCN noise rate <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}=\eta_{i}^{*}=1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}=\eta_{i}^{*}=1/4</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that in this setting, all variants of the MDL problem coincide.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our argument relies on the observation that the testing problem (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) is <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">necessary</span> for MDL. That is, any algorithm that solves MDL can be readily converted into a solver for (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>). Specifically, we can treat the MDL algorithm as a black box to learn proxies for the optimal classifier, and then use them to solve the testing problem with only <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\mathinner{\left(\log\mathinner{\left(1/\delta\right)}/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\mathinner{\left(\log\mathinner{\left(1/\delta\right)}/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> additional samples per distribution. We formally describe this reduction in <a href="#alg6" title="In 5 MDL Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and derive its sample complexity in the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg6" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 6</span> </span> MDL to MSHT</figcaption>
<div id="alg6.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg6.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg6.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
          Function class <math id="alg6.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, distributions <math id="alg6.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\dots,P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\dots,P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, functions <math id="alg6.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},\dots,f_{k}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},\dots,f_{k}\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}\mathcal{X}\to\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, parameters <math id="alg6.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}\in\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}\in\mathinner{\left(0,1\right)}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, MDL algorithm <math id="alg6.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> with sample complexity <math id="alg6.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg6.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg6.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
          <math id="alg6.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(\hat{f}_{1},\dots,\hat{f}_{k}\right)}\leftarrow\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(\hat{f}_{1},\dots,\hat{f}_{k}\right)}\leftarrow\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> where we run <math id="alg6.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="alg6.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon/48,\delta/3\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon/48,\delta/3\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> rounds.

</div>
<div id="alg6.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg6.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg6.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg6.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg6.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg6.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg6.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
            Sample <math id="alg6.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}\overset{iid}{\sim}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="alg6.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{L}}\mathinner{\left(1/4,\epsilon,\delta\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝖫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert S_{i}\right\rvert}=T_{\mathsf{L}}\mathinner{\left(1/4,\epsilon,\delta\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg6.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg6.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg6.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg6.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\emperr\mathinner{\left(f_{i};S_{i}\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(\hat{f}_{i};S_{i}\right)}\right\rvert}\leq\epsilon/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>err</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\!\left\lvert\emperr\mathinner{\left(f_{i};S_{i}\right)}-\emperr\mathinner{\left(\hat{f}_{i};S_{i}\right)}\right\rvert}\leq\epsilon/3</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg6.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg6.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg6.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
             <math id="alg6.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{D}_{i}\leftarrow\mathsf{YES}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝖸𝖤𝖲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{D}_{i}\leftarrow\mathsf{YES}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg6.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg6.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
            <span id="alg6.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="alg6.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg6.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
            
            
             <math id="alg6.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{D}_{i}\leftarrow\mathsf{NO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝖭𝖮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{D}_{i}\leftarrow\mathsf{NO}</annotation></semantics></math>.
  
</div>
<div id="alg6.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg6.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
            
            
          Decision vector <math id="alg6.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(\mathsf{D}_{1},\dots,\mathsf{D}_{k}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(\mathsf{D}_{1},\dots,\mathsf{D}_{k}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(MDL to MSHT upper bound)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#alg6" title="In 5 MDL Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, under an MDL algorithm <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> with sample complexity <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, solves the (<a href="#S4.Ex46" title="Equation MSHT ‣ 4.3 Multi-Distribution SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MSHT</span></a>) problem with sample complexity</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx48" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathcal{A}\to\mathsf{MSHT}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}=T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\frac{\epsilon}{48},\frac{\delta}{3}\right)}+\frac{192k\log\mathinner{\left(6/\delta\right)}}{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝖬𝖲𝖧𝖳</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>48</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>192</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathcal{A}\to\mathsf{MSHT}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}=T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\frac{\epsilon}{48},\frac{\delta}{3}\right)}+\frac{192k\log\mathinner{\left(6/\delta\right)}}{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that our proposed strategies for (<a href="#S1.Ex3" title="Equation MDL-RCN ‣ RCN. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-RCN</span></a>) and (<a href="#S1.Ex4" title="Equation MDL-MM ‣ Minimax. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-MM</span></a>) achieve a sample complexity of <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\min\mathinner{\left\{dk/\epsilon,d/\epsilon+k/\epsilon^{2}\right\}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\min\mathinner{\left\{dk/\epsilon,d/\epsilon+k/\epsilon^{2}\right\}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. We now show that this rate is optimal, up to logarithmic factors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(MDL lower bound)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leq 0.01/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leq 0.01/3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 384\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>384</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 384\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\mathinner{\left\{d,1/\epsilon\right\}}\geq 4\cdot 10^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\mathinner{\left\{d,1/\epsilon\right\}}\geq 4\cdot 10^{7}</annotation></semantics></math>. Any MDL algorithm <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> requires a sample size of</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx49" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}=\Omega\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon}+k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}=\Omega\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon}+k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>(<a href="#S1.Ex5" title="Equation MDL-Mass ‣ Massart. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-Mass</span></a>) Lower Bound</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now turn our attention to the most challenging MDL variant, (<a href="#S1.Ex5" title="Equation MDL-Mass ‣ Massart. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-Mass</span></a>). Recall that in <a href="#S5" title="5 MDL Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we established a lower bound of <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon+k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{1/\epsilon^{2},d/\epsilon\right\}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\mathinner{\left(d/\epsilon+k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{1/\epsilon^{2},d/\epsilon\right\}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\gtrsim 1/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≳</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\gtrsim 1/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, the second term saturates at <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k/\epsilon^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k/\epsilon^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, losing the multiplicative dependence on <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show that the stricter requirements of (<a href="#S1.Ex5" title="Equation MDL-Mass ‣ Massart. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-Mass</span></a>) imply a stronger lower bound that retains a multiplicative factor of <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\sqrt{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\sqrt{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, even in the large-<math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> regime. To prove this, we follow the same recipe as in <a href="#S5" title="5 MDL Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>: we introduce an auxiliary testing problem, denoted by (<a href="#S6.Ex55" title="Equation SHT-Mass ‣ 6.1 Massart Testing ‣ 6 () Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT-Mass</span></a>), and demonstrate that any algorithm solving (<a href="#S1.Ex5" title="Equation MDL-Mass ‣ Massart. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-Mass</span></a>) must implicitly solve an instance of this testing problem for each distribution.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Massart Testing</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We begin by defining an auxiliary testing problem. Consider a pair of random variables <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(X,Y\right)}\in\llbracket d\rrbracket\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(X,Y\right)}\in\llbracket d\rrbracket\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the marginal distribution of <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S6.EGx50" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{X}\mathinner{\left(x\right)}=\begin{cases}1-\epsilon,&amp;x=0\\
\epsilon/d,&amp;x\in\mathinner{\left[d\right]}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{X}\mathinner{\left(x\right)}=\begin{cases}1-\epsilon,&amp;x=0\\
\epsilon/d,&amp;x\in\mathinner{\left[d\right]}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and the conditional distributions are <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y|X=x\sim\Ber\mathinner{\left(q_{x}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Ber</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y|X=x\sim\Ber\mathinner{\left(q_{x}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The vector of biases <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}=\mathinner{\left(q_{x}\right)}_{x=0}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐪</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}=\mathinner{\left(q_{x}\right)}_{x=0}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S6.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unknown</span>, but constrained such that <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0}\in\mathinner{\left[0,0.49\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.49</mn><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0}\in\mathinner{\left[0,0.49\right]}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{x}\in\mathinner{\left[0,0.49\right]}\cup\mathinner{\left[0.51,1\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.49</mn><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mn>0.51</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{x}\in\mathinner{\left[0,0.49\right]}\cup\mathinner{\left[0.51,1\right]}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathinner{\left[d\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathinner{\left[d\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</p>
<table id="S6.EGx51" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex54"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{\mathbf{q}}\coloneqq\frac{\epsilon}{d}\sum_{x\in\llbracket d\rrbracket\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}q_{x}\geq 1/2}\mathinner{\left(2q_{x}-1\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝐪</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{\mathbf{q}}\coloneqq\frac{\epsilon}{d}\sum_{x\in\llbracket d\rrbracket\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}q_{x}\geq 1/2}\mathinner{\left(2q_{x}-1\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The goal is to draw i.i.d. <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(X,Y\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(X,Y\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> pairs and determine which of the following two hypotheses holds:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex55" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S6.Ex55X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex55X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{$H_{0}$: If $q_{x}=0.465$ for all $x\in\llbracket d\rrbracket$, output $\mathsf{YES}$ with probability at least $1-\delta$.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext>: If </mtext><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.465</mn></mrow><mtext> for all </mtext><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow></mrow><mtext>, output </mtext><mi>𝖸𝖤𝖲</mi><mtext> with probability at least </mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{$H_{0}$: If $q_{x}=0.465$ for all $x\in\llbracket d\rrbracket$, output $\mathsf{YES}$ with probability at least $1-\delta$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(SHT-Mass)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.Ex55Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex55Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{$H_{1}$: If $\Delta_{\mathbf{q}}\geq 0.3\epsilon$, output $\mathsf{NO}$ with probability at least $1-\delta$.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mtext>: If </mtext><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐪</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>0.3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext>, output </mtext><mi>𝖭𝖮</mi><mtext> with probability at least </mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{$H_{1}$: If $\Delta_{\mathbf{q}}\geq 0.3\epsilon$, output $\mathsf{NO}$ with probability at least $1-\delta$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the analysis below, we demonstrate that solving (<a href="#S6.Ex55" title="Equation SHT-Mass ‣ 6.1 Massart Testing ‣ 6 () Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT-Mass</span></a>) requires a polynomial dependence on <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, even when <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\gg 1/\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≫</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\gg 1/\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, standing in sharp contrast to the behavior of (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(SHT-Mass lower bound)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leq 1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leq 1/4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 1750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1750</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 1750</annotation></semantics></math>. Any algorithm <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> that solves (<a href="#S6.Ex55" title="Equation SHT-Mass ‣ 6.1 Massart Testing ‣ 6 () Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT-Mass</span></a>) requires a sample size of</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx52" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex56"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}\geq\frac{\sqrt{d}}{2\sqrt{2}\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}\geq\frac{\sqrt{d}}{2\sqrt{2}\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch of <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1 (SHT-Mass lower bound). ‣ 6.1 Massart Testing ‣ 6 () Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Our ultimate goal is to construct bias vector <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, for each <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, that falls under hypothesis <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and such that <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are difficult to distinguish. To start, we set the <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>th coordinate of each to <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.465" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.465</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.465</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the only informative samples are those in which <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathinner{\left[d\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathinner{\left[d\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then construct priors <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and sample the remaining biases i.i.d. from <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> in a way that the resulting <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> with high probability. More importantly, we also make sure that the first moments of <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> match, which ensures that <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> has the same mean under both hypotheses. Since the marginal <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the hypothesis, it is thus necessary to exploit the relationship between <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. This can only be accomplished once we observe repeated values of <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left[d\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left[d\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>, which takes <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\mathinner{\left(\sqrt{d}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\mathinner{\left(\sqrt{d}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> samples. The <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\mathinner{\left(\sqrt{d}/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\mathinner{\left(\sqrt{d}/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> bound then follows from the fact that it takes <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\mathinner{\left(1/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> samples to obtain a single point in <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left[d\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left[d\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We emphasize that the moment matching argument requires varying the noise levels, preventing us from fixing the noise as in the (<a href="#S4.Ex32" title="Equation SHT ‣ 4 Structured Hypothesis Testing ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT</span></a>) setup. Consequently, this construction is specific to (<a href="#S1.Ex5" title="Equation MDL-Mass ‣ Massart. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-Mass</span></a>) and does not extend to the other MDL variants.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations of the (<a href="#S6.Ex55" title="Equation SHT-Mass ‣ 6.1 Massart Testing ‣ 6 () Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT-Mass</span></a>) construction.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is nothing inherently special about the <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{d}</annotation></semantics></math> dependence. One could employ higher-order moment matching and polynomial methods to improve the exponent on <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>; see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. In fact, with a more delicate analysis, our construction may plausibly yield a lower bound whose <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-exponent exhibits a finer-grained dependence on the noise parameters. However, extending this all the way to a linear rate is impossible with the current (<a href="#S6.Ex55" title="Equation SHT-Mass ‣ 6.1 Massart Testing ‣ 6 () Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT-Mass</span></a>) construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To see why, observe that to obtain a constant <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathbf{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐪</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathbf{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> gap, a constant fraction of the coordinates must be heavy (i.e., <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{x}\geq 0.51" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.51</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{x}\geq 0.51</annotation></semantics></math>) under <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This creates a vulnerability: an algorithm need only detect <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">one</span> such heavy coordinate to reject <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=O\mathinner{\left(1\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=O\mathinner{\left(1\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of samples required to reliably distinguish between <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Ber\mathinner{\left(0.49\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ber</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0.49</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Ber\mathinner{\left(0.49\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Ber\mathinner{\left(0.51\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Ber</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0.51</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Ber\mathinner{\left(0.51\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider a learner that samples until it observes <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>-collisions on a small, constant number of unique coordinates. Under <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, at least one of these coordinates will be heavy with high probability, allowing the learner to identify it. Since finding <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>-collisions requires only <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\mathinner{\left(d^{1-1/C}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\mathinner{\left(d^{1-1/C}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> samples, the hardness of this instance is strictly sublinear in <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We note that while this does not rule out the existence of other hard instances achieving a linear dependence, constructing one that yields an MDL lower bound via a similar reduction is nontrivial. This is because our choice of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (containing only light biases) is critical for ensuring that the resulting hypothesis class maintains a fixed VC dimension when extended to multiple distributions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Lower Bound</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we construct an MDL instance that inherits the (<a href="#S6.Ex55" title="Equation SHT-Mass ‣ 6.1 Massart Testing ‣ 6 () Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">SHT-Mass</span></a>) difficulty. Let the covariate space be <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\coloneqq\llbracket kd+k-1\rrbracket" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\coloneqq\llbracket kd+k-1\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math>, which we partition into <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint blocks of size <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+1</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th block by <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{i}\coloneqq\mathinner{\left\{\mathinner{\left(i-1\right)}\mathinner{\left(d+1\right)}+x\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}x\in\llbracket d\rrbracket\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{i}\coloneqq\mathinner{\left\{\mathinner{\left(i-1\right)}\mathinner{\left(d+1\right)}+x\mathrel{\mathop{\mathchar 58\relax}}x\in\llbracket d\rrbracket\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define the hypothesis class <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the set of binary functions consisting of:</p>
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The zero function <math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All functions that equal <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> precisely on a subset of <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{i}\backslash\mathinner{\left\{\mathinner{\left(i-1\right)}\mathinner{\left(d+1\right)}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{i}\backslash\mathinner{\left\{\mathinner{\left(i-1\right)}\mathinner{\left(d+1\right)}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., we exclude the first coordinate) for some <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\VC\mathinner{\left(\mathcal{F}\right)}=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>VCdim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\VC\mathinner{\left(\mathcal{F}\right)}=d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we define distributions <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},\dots,P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},\dots,P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\times\mathinner{\left\{0,1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> with marginal PMFs</p>
<table id="S6.EGx53" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex57"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{i}\mathinner{\left(X=x\right)}=\begin{cases}1-\epsilon,&amp;x=\mathinner{\left(i-1\right)}\mathinner{\left(d+1\right)}\\
\epsilon/d,&amp;x\in\mathinner{\left\{\mathinner{\left(i-1\right)}\mathinner{\left(d+1\right)}+1,\dots,i\mathinner{\left(d+1\right)}-1\right\}}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{i}\mathinner{\left(X=x\right)}=\begin{cases}1-\epsilon,&amp;x=\mathinner{\left(i-1\right)}\mathinner{\left(d+1\right)}\\
\epsilon/d,&amp;x\in\mathinner{\left\{\mathinner{\left(i-1\right)}\mathinner{\left(d+1\right)}+1,\dots,i\mathinner{\left(d+1\right)}-1\right\}}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">That is, the probability mass is distributed as <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathinner{\left(1-\epsilon,\epsilon/d,\dots,\epsilon/d\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathinner{\left(1-\epsilon,\epsilon/d,\dots,\epsilon/d\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> on the corresponding <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, we require the label noise to satisfy the Massart constraint with respect to some target <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.EGx54" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex58"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\eta_{i}^{*}\mathinner{\left(x\right)}\coloneqq P_{i}\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y|X=x\right)}\leq 0.49\quad\forall x\in\mathcal{X},i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.49</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\eta_{i}^{*}\mathinner{\left(x\right)}\coloneqq P_{i}\mathinner{\left(f^{*}\mathinner{\left(X\right)}\neq Y|X=x\right)}\leq 0.49\quad\forall x\in\mathcal{X},i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Recall that while the optimal risk values <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}^{*}=\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P_{i}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}^{*}=\err\mathinner{\left(f^{*};P_{i}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> are unknown, we know the noise upper bounds <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{1}=\dots=\eta_{k}=0.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{1}=\dots=\eta_{k}=0.49</annotation></semantics></math>. The goal of (<a href="#S1.Ex5" title="Equation MDL-Mass ‣ Massart. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-Mass</span></a>) is to sample from the distributions and output hypotheses <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{1},\dots,\hat{f}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{1},\dots,\hat{f}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</p>
<table id="S6.EGx55" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex59"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\mathinner{\left(\max_{i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}}\mathinner{\left\{\err\mathinner{\left(\hat{f}_{i};P_{i}\right)}-\eta_{i}^{*}\right\}}\leq\epsilon\right)}\geq 1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>err</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\mathinner{\left(\max_{i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}}\mathinner{\left\{\err\mathinner{\left(\hat{f}_{i};P_{i}\right)}-\eta_{i}^{*}\right\}}\leq\epsilon\right)}\geq 1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">The following theorem establishes that any algorithm solving this problem must incur the multiplicative cost discussed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>((<a href="#S1.Ex5" title="Equation MDL-Mass ‣ Massart. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-Mass</span></a>) lower bound)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\leq 0.1/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\leq 0.1/3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 7\cdot 10^{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 7\cdot 10^{10}</annotation></semantics></math>. Any algorithm <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> that solves (<a href="#S1.Ex5" title="Equation MDL-Mass ‣ Massart. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-Mass</span></a>) in this setup requires a sample size of</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx56" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex60"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}=\Omega\mathinner{\left(\frac{k\sqrt{d}}{\epsilon}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\mathcal{A}}\mathinner{\left(\epsilon,\delta\right)}=\Omega\mathinner{\left(\frac{k\sqrt{d}}{\epsilon}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By combining this result with the general lower bound from <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (MDL lower bound). ‣ 5 MDL Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, we can conclude that, under a constant noise upper bound of <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{i}\leq 0.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{i}\leq 0.49</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathinner{\left[k\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>, a successful algorithm for (<a href="#S1.Ex5" title="Equation MDL-Mass ‣ Massart. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-Mass</span></a>) requires a sample size of at least</p>
<table id="S6.EGx57" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex61"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Omega\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon}+k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}+\frac{k\sqrt{d}}{\epsilon}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Omega\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon}+k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}+\frac{k\sqrt{d}}{\epsilon}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we investigated the limits of multi-distribution learning under bounded label noise, asking whether the fast rates achievable in the single-distribution setting extend to the multi-distribution regime. We formulated three distinct variants of the problem based on the benchmarks they target: the known noise upper bounds, the minimax error, and the true unknown optimal errors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the first two variants, we devised strategies that achieve sample complexities <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{\epsilon+\eta_{i}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{\epsilon+\eta_{i}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\epsilon+\eta^{*}\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon\mathinner{\left(1-2\eta\right)}}+\frac{k\mathinner{\left(\epsilon+\eta^{*}\right)}}{\epsilon^{2}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Through a reduction to multi-distribution hypothesis testing, we proved that these are optimal even under constant noise, ruling out a fast rate of <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}\mathinner{\left(\frac{d+k}{\epsilon}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. This highlights a fundamental departure from the realizable and agnostic MDL complexities, where the overhead for scaling to <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> distributions is merely additive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we showed that the third variant is strictly harder by constructing a lower bound of <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\mathinner{\left(k\sqrt{d}/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\mathinner{\left(k\sqrt{d}/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> via moment matching. This establishes a statistical separation between RCN and Massart noise in the MDL setting and reveals that for this objective, the penalty for learning multiple distributions is multiplicative rather than additive.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Future Directions.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work leaves several compelling avenues for future research.</p>
<ol id="S7.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Centralized MDL:</span> We focused on personalized learning, where the algorithm outputs a distinct hypothesis <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each distribution. It remains unclear whether similar rates apply to the centralized setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, where the learner is constrained to produce a single common hypothesis <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}</annotation></semantics></math> with small error across all distributions simultaneously.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Noise-dependent Lower Bounds:</span> While our lower bounds establish optimality in the constant-noise regime, our analysis does not fully characterize the complexity as a function of the noise rate.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Closing the Gap for (<a href="#S1.Ex5" title="Equation MDL-Mass ‣ Massart. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-Mass</span></a>):</span> A tight sample complexity bound for the hardest variant, (<a href="#S1.Ex5" title="Equation MDL-Mass ‣ Massart. ‣ 1.1 Problem Setup ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">MDL-Mass</span></a>), remains unknown. While we established a lower bound of</p>
<table id="S7.EGx58" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex62"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Omega\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon}+k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}+\frac{k\sqrt{d}}{\epsilon}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Omega\mathinner{\left(\frac{d}{\epsilon}+k\cdot\min\mathinner{\left\{\frac{1}{\epsilon^{2}},\frac{d}{\epsilon}\right\}}+\frac{k\sqrt{d}}{\epsilon}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">it is unclear whether the true complexity scales linearly as <math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\mathinner{\left(kd/\epsilon\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\mathinner{\left(kd/\epsilon\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Determining the precise interplay between <math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in this setting likely requires a fundamentally different construction, as discussed in <a href="#S6" title="6 () Lower Bound ‣ Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
